Each part has a different responsibility.

The shared state describes the general condition and history of a region. It continues to provide the common foundation that allows different gameplay systems to interact.

The modal channels describe the kinds of influence accumulated in that region. They allow two chunks with similar general state values to have different histories and meanings.

The provenance metadata records where an influence came from, which system produced it, and what kind of event occurred. This preserves causal identity that would otherwise disappear when many events are combined into a few numbers.

The interpretation layer determines what those differences mean to gameplay. A fire channel does not automatically cause a particular mob to spawn; the relevant actuator decides whether that channel, combined with the other regional conditions, warrants the effect.
