UTD could add a different kind of progression: the region itself can improve through what players do there.

Imagine three farms.

Farm A — Newly established

The substrate has little accumulated organization. Crops grow at their normal baseline rate, and the area has little history of sustained productive activity.

Farm B — Stable and organized

Repeated cultivation, suitable infrastructure, and stabilizing assets have helped the region establish a favorable condition. If the mechanics support it, crops might grow faster, substrate disturbances might dissipate more gently, and useful regional effects might become more reliable.

Farm C — Overloaded or destabilized

Excessive industrial disturbance, hostile magic, or other harmful influences have pushed the region away from its favorable condition. Growth may slow, anomalies may appear, or recovery may become harder.

The important part is not the exact crop-growth numbers. It's that these locations can differ because of their histories, not simply because one has a different biome or a better fertilizer.

That gives the player a new reason to care about a place. Instead of only asking, “What can I build here?”, they can ask, “What kind of region am I turning this place into?”
2. What SS should mean

I'd define SS provisionally as a regional organization or stability reserve: a measure of how strongly a region maintains a favorable, organized condition against disturbances.

It should not merely mean “low tension.” Those are different ideas.

    Tension or disturbance describes what is happening to the region.

    Memory describes some aspect of its accumulated history.

    Depth describes another dimension of its established substrate state.

    Modal signatures distinguish the kinds of influences shaping it.

    Stability SS would describe the region's ability to preserve or recover a favorable organization under those influences.

That final distinction is the reason to consider adding it.

A quiet, untouched chunk might have very low disturbance but also very little established organization. A productive, well-maintained region could have substantial history and a strong ability to withstand ordinary disturbances. A heavily overloaded region might have high disturbance and declining stability.

Those are three different states. A good model should be able to represent them if they produce meaningful differences in gameplay.
3. Where SS would actually be useful in UTD

I see four strong use cases, and one particularly important one for your multi-aspect extension.

1. Regional development and productive assets

Your existing resource and asset systems could do more than provide a temporary bonus. Assets could contribute to a region's ability to maintain a favorable state. Sustained productive activity might establish a stable agricultural region, while neglect or damaging disturbances gradually erode it.

Why it matters: Players gain a reason to invest in the long-term condition of a place, not just its immediate output.

2. Settlements that resist disturbances

Two otherwise similar villages could have different resilience. A well-established settlement with suitable stabilizing assets might recover more effectively after repeated supernatural or environmental disturbances.

Why it matters: It gives settlement building, protection, and maintenance a persistent consequence beyond the usual structures and defenses.

3. Stabilizing magical or industrial regions

A region with a strong arcane signature might not need to become harmless. Instead, high stability could make its anomalies more predictable or controllable, while low stability makes them volatile.

Why it matters: Stability becomes a meaningful trade-off. Players can deliberately cultivate a powerful but dangerous region and then invest in controlling it.

4. Multi-aspect signatures and hybrid regions

An industrial-arcane region could have a distinct modal signature and also possess high or low stability. The signature tells the game what kinds of influences are present; SS helps determine whether those influences produce controlled, persistent behavior or volatile disturbances.

Why it matters: It adds another dimension to the world state without requiring every possible industrial-magic combination to become a separate hard-coded region type.

The fourth use case is the most compelling architectural reason to keep SS under consideration. Modal channels describe what kind of influence exists. Stability describes how well the region maintains its organization under that influence. Those are complementary questions.
4. Can the existing variables already do this?

Possibly, at least partially. This is the main reason I would not implement SS immediately.

The current UTD design already has activity, memory, depth, rest, and resource-related state. Our earlier exploratory stability experiment also showed that a separate variable could be used to reduce the effective response to incoming disturbances. But that was a temporary test harness, not an implemented or validated runtime mechanic.

The correct test is whether the existing state can distinguish the situations we want.

