Where I would draw the line for UTD

I would divide the current ideas into three groups.

1. Essential to UTD's identity

    A shared, persistent regional substrate.

    Spatial influence: activity in one location can affect nearby regions.

    History-dependent behavior, so regions don't respond only to what is happening right now.

    Gameplay actuators that turn the field into observable consequences.

    A reliable connection between the implemented equations, recorded telemetry, and the behavior players see.

Without these, UTD risks being a collection of counters and scripted effects rather than a coherent regional dynamics system. These are the foundations to establish first.

2. Potentially necessary extensions

Multi-aspect substrate: This becomes important if UTD is intended to support different mods whose influences must remain distinguishable. A scalar field that treats fire, ice, machinery, and magic as equivalent disturbance may not be sufficient for that goal. But multiple channels should be added when the current system demonstrably cannot preserve the distinctions required by gameplay.

Stability SS: This becomes important if a developed region needs to withstand disturbances better than an otherwise equivalent region, and the existing variables cannot represent that difference. If memory, depth, and asset state already produce the required behavior without awkward rules, another variable may be redundant.

Provenance: If quests or factions must distinguish an industrial accident from a magical disaster, the game needs some way to preserve cause. That does not automatically mean adding another continuous field variable. Event records, source tags, or regional provenance metadata might do the job more cheaply.

3. Things that should not be added just because they sound useful

    A separate variable for every gameplay concept.

    A new subsystem whenever an actuator needs special handling.

    More channels before there is a concrete interaction that requires them.

    Additional complexity to make the theory appear more sophisticated.

    More experiments that measure internal correlations without establishing whether the implementation matches its declared equations.

These additions need a specific problem to solve. Otherwise, defer them.
The crucial distinction: a new capability versus a new implementation detail

Take the stability variable as an example.

Suppose UTD already has activity, memory, depth, resource assets, and a way to represent an organized region. If those mechanisms can already make a well-developed farm more productive and more resistant to disruption, then adding SS might merely duplicate existing information.

But suppose two regions have the same current activity, memory, depth, and resource assets, yet one has developed a persistent ability to resist ordinary disturbances while the other has not. If that difference matters to gameplay and cannot be represented reliably with the existing state, SS may be a genuine missing mechanism.

That is a real architectural distinction. It is not a matter of whether the extra variable sounds elegant.

The same test applies to multi-aspect dynamics. If the only requirement is that fire causes damage and ice freezes water, ordinary mod-specific rules may suffice. If UTD must remember that a region has been shaped by both fire and ice, allow their influences to interact, and let downstream systems react to that history, a richer representation may be justified.

The extension earns its place when a concrete behavior requires it—not simply because the architecture can accommodate it.
What this means for UTD right now

There is an important complication: the existing implementation still has unresolved mathematical and experimental questions, including the discrepancy between the declared memory update rule and the reported telemetry. That does not prove the core concept is bad. It means we should not use current experimental claims as if they already establish every aspect of the system.

I would proceed in this order:

    Establish the core. Verify that the runtime implements its declared dynamics and that telemetry measures those dynamics correctly. Separate genuine runtime defects from mismatches in the experimental lab.

    Demonstrate a complete gameplay loop. Show that player activity changes a region, the region retains meaningful history, and that history later produces a noticeable gameplay consequence. A mathematically interesting field with no compelling gameplay consequences is not enough.

    Test the extensions against specific failures of the core. Use matched experiments to determine whether stability adds resilience, multi-aspect state preserves distinctions the scalar field loses, and provenance enables decisions that would otherwise be impossible.

    Integrate only the extensions that pass. Keep the others as documented proposals rather than allowing every promising idea to become part of the production architecture.

One more point matters: the architecture does not have to implement every possible mechanic before UTD can be a legitimate project. A coherent core with three excellent gameplay consequences is stronger than a sprawling framework with twenty poorly verified ones.
My verdict

I think the multi-aspect extension addresses a real limitation if cross-mod, cause-sensitive interactions are central to the product you want to build. I think stability SS addresses a real potential limitation, but its necessity remains unproven because existing variables and resource assets may already be capable of expressing the behavior. Provenance is a real requirement for causal storytelling, but it may belong in metadata rather than the continuous substrate itself.

So I would not abandon these ideas, and I would not add them all now.

The test I would hold UTD to is simple: Can we name the player-visible behavior, demonstrate why the current architecture cannot produce it adequately, and show that the proposed extension solves that specific limitation? If we cannot answer all three, it stays a proposal.

That is how UTD avoids both mistakes: becoming so minimal that it cannot deliver its vision, and becoming so elaborate that the architecture itself becomes the project.
